      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "This study reviews the outcomes of reverse shoulder arthroplasty (RSA) and introduces a novel method of measuring changes in the center-of-rotation (COR) of the shoulder joint post-operatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We performed 10 reverse shoulders in 2011. Patients were evaluated pre-operatively and post-operatively using the Constant Score. The COR of the shoulder was determined pre- and post-operatively on radiographs and the differences were analysed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was significant improvement in Constant Scores post-operatively with reduction in pain and increase in strength. Medialisation but not distalisation of the COR was achieved."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Longer follow-up studies and studies on survivalship are required."
        }
      ]},
